\begin_layout Section

\shape italic
G_RX_BUF_SIZE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CTU CAN FD stores frames received on CAN bus to a FIFO called RX buffer that is located within the CTU CAN FD.
 The FIFO is implemented as synchronous dual-port RAM memory with no forwarding in case of simultaneous read and write to the same address.
 The width of the memory word is 32.
 The depth is given by 
\series bold
\shape slanted
G_RX_BUF_SIZE
\series default
\shape default
.
 The memory is implementd in 
\shape italic
i_dp_inf_ram.vhd
\shape default
.
 The memory is reset to zero when 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_RESET_BUF_RAMS=
\series default
\shape default
true.
 Whether the memory is configured to be reset or not,
 the memory is always written before it can be read by the user.
 If memory has undefined data in it and SW attempts to read it,
 the undefined data are gated.
 For design-for-test implications see 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Design-for-Test"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As the area of the RX buffer is significant,
 it is recommended to use appropriat size.
 To find out the appropriate size,
 understanding how RX buffer works is important.
 On CAN bus,
 the frames can be received back-to-back,
 without any idle state.
 CTU CAN FD stores these frames to RX buffer until the RX buffer is full.
 If the RX buffer overflows,
 the received frame is lost.
 When SW reads the frame from RX buffer,
 it is discared from the RX buffer.
 Therefore size of the RX buffer is inversly proportional to the worst-case reaction time of SW in the system that integrates CTU CAN FD.
 This depends on factors such as:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
HW latency of memory accesses made by the SW.
 Latency of access to e.g.
 a PCIe card with CTU CAN FD on it is very different compared to latency of an SoC bus if CTU CAN FD is an on-die peripheral.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Operating system scheduler latency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Delay of ISR routine to read out the received frame.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A 64-byte CAN FD frame spans 20 words in RX buffer.
 Therefore using 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_RX_BUF_SIZE=
\series default
\shape default
32,
 allows storing only one 64-byte CAN FD frame.
 Reception of second 64-byte CAN FD frame will overflow the RX buffer if the SW does not manage to read out the first frame fast enough.
 To analyze RX buffer behavior we use following terminology:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $t_{NOM}$
\end_inset

 - Nominal bit-rate bit duration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $t_{DAT}$
\end_inset

 - Data bit-rate bit duration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $t_{SW}$
\end_inset

 - Worst case SW reaction time (including HW propagation,
 ISR,
 scheduling latency and frame readout) to read CAN frame
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where all the values must have the same unit of time,
 then 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_RX_BUF_SIZE
\series default
\shape default
 should be set to meet following:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{t_{SW}}{33*t_{NOM}+86*t_{DAT}}*20>\mbox{\textit{\textbf{G\_RX\_BUF\_SIZE}}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Example:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $t_{NOM}=2\:us$
\end_inset

 (500 Kbit/s)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $t_{DAT}=0.5\:us$
\end_inset

 (2 Mbit/s)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $t_{SW}=200\:us$
\end_inset

 (should be safely reachable on real-time OS)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{200}{33*2+86*0.5}*20>\mbox{\textit{\textbf{G\_RX\_BUF\_SIZE}}}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
36>\mbox{\textit{\textbf{G\_RX\_BUF\_SIZE}}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore we set 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_RX_BUF_SIZE=
\series default
\shape default
64.
 Note that on non-realtime operating systems the scheduler delay will be significantly higher than 200 us.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section

\shape italic
G_FILT_*_EN
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CTU CAN FD can filter received RX frames by 3 bit filters (A,B,C) and one range filter.
 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_FILT_*_EN 
\series default
\shape default
generics either enable or disable these filter to be synthesized into the design.
 When enabled,
 the filters can be configured at run-time to receive frames whose CAN identifier matches a mask (bit filter),
 or are within decimal range (range filter).
 Filters add little HW area compared to size of RX buffer or number of TXT Buffers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section

\shape italic
G_TRAFFIC_CTRS_EN
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CTU CAN FD can measure transmitted and received frames in HW.
 When 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_TRAFFIC_CTRS_EN
\series default
\shape default
=true,
 CTU CAN XL contains these counters.
 There is one counter for received frames and one for trasnmitted frames.
 Each counter is 32-bit wide.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section

\shape italic
G_TXT_BUF_CNT
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CTU CAN FD transmits CAN frames from TXT buffers.
 Each TXT buffer can hold one 64-byte CAN frame to be transmitted.
 Each TXT buffer contains a synchronous dual-port RAM memory with no forwarding in case of simultaneous read and write to the same address.
 Width of the memory is 32-bit and depth is 21 words.
 The memory is implementd in 
\shape italic
dp_inf_ram.vhd
\shape default
.
 The memory is reset to zero when 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_RESET_BUF_RAMS
\series default
\shape default
=true
\series bold
\shape italic
 
\series default
\shape default
.
 It is responsibility of SW driver to make sure the memory is initialized when using corresponding TXT buffer to transmit a frame.
 For design-for-test implications of TXT buffer memory see 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Design-for-Test"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section

\shape italic
G_TEST_REGS_EN
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CTU CAN FD supports functional DFT testability of TXT buffer and RX buffer memories.
 When 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_TEST_REGS_EN
\series default
\shape default
 = true,
 this feature is present.
 For more details on DFT,
 see 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Design-for-Test"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section

\shape italic
G_PARITY_EN
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CTU CAN FD supports parity on TXT buffer and RX buffer memories.
 When 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_PARITY_EN
\series default
\shape default
=true,
 the parity is enabled.
 If enabled,
 there exists a parity bit for each word of TXT buffer and RX buffer memories.
 The extra bit is not held in the memory itself,
 but in side-array of flip-flops.
 When the memory is written,
 the parity bit is set.
 When the memory is read,
 the bit is checked and can notify SW of bit-flip.
 The parity can only detect single event effects,
 there is no support for correction.
 Using parity is usefull when synthesizing the design with TMR (each flip-flop is trippled),
 but wanting to use actual RAM blocks for TXT and RX buffers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section

\shape italic
G_RESET_BUF_RAMS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_RESET_BUF_RAMS
\series default
\shape default
=true,
 CTU CAN FD TXT and RX buffer RAMs have asynchronous reset.
 This is usefull to:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Prevent inferring SRAM blocks on FPGAs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Forcing the memory be out of flip-flops that have set and reset,
 and are therefore ellegible for scan-chain insertion.
 For more details on design-for-test see 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "chap:Design-for-Test"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section

\shape italic
G_ACTIVE_TS_BITS
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CTU CAN FD can sample timestamp of received frames or do time-triggered transmission of CAN FD frames.
 Such time-base shall be provided by the system that integrates CTU CAN FD.
 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_ACTIVE_TS_BITS
\series default
\shape default
 shall be set to number of active bits of such time-base minus 1.
 E.g.
 when 
\color red
timestamp
\color inherit
 input is driven by 32 bit counter and its upper 32-bits are driven by zero,
 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_ACTIVE_TS_BITS 
\series default
\shape default
shall be set to 31.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section

\shape italic
G_TECHNOLOGY
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CTU CAN FD supports either ASIC or FPGA synthesis.
 The difference between ASIC and FPGA synthesis is the use of hand-instantiated clock-gaters (see 
\shape italic
clk_gate.vhd
\shape default
) for gating clock to:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Memory registers when they are not being accessed
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
RX buffer RAM when it is not being accessed
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
TXT buffer RAMs when they are not being accessed
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Clocking and Reset
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "chap:Clocking-and-Reset"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Clocking
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CTU CAN FD has single clock domain - system clock.
 The clock signal is called differently depending on the memory bus wrapper used (
\color red
clk_sys
\color inherit
,
 
\color red
clk
\color inherit
,
 
\color red
hclk
\color inherit
,
 
\color red
aclk
\color inherit
),
 but it is always the same clock.
 The CTU CAN FD design is fully synchronous.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Clock tolerance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CAN bus bit timing requires certain oscillator tolerances to be met by the oscillator that clocks the CAN bit timing logic.
 For more details see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "key-3"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
 Since CTU CAN FD uses single clock for CAN bit timing as well as memory bus,
 the same tolerance requirements apply on the memory bus clocking.
 Therefore it is unsuitable to use on-chip CMOS oscillator as source of clock for CTU CAN FD.
 If you absolutely must use on-chip oscillator to clock the memory bus infrastracture,
 CTU CAN FD shall be wrapped by CDC that resynchronizes the memory bus to another clock domain with appropriate clock source tolerance that is used to clock CTU CAN FD.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Clock architecture
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All flip-clops in CTU CAN FD are positive-edge triggered flip flops.
 CTU CAN FD does not:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
multiply or divide clocks
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
multiplex clocks
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
use 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

clock-as-data
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

data-as-clock
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (e.g.
 ripple counters)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
has no generated clocks
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CTU CAN FD extensively uses clock enables that are written such that synthesizer can infer flip-flops with enable pin or clock-gates.
 When 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_TECHNOLOGY
\series default
\shape default
=0,
 CTU CAN FD uses manually instantiated clock gates to save dynamic power.
 These clock-gates are latch+AND style clock gates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Signal synchronizers
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Signal-synchronizers"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CTU CAN FD uses single two flip-flop signal synchronizer (
\shape italic
sig_sync.vhd
\shape default
) to synchronize input data from 
\color red
can_rx
\color inherit
 before processing them.
 Extending the synchronizer with third flip-flop (e.g.
 due to MTBF in your target technology),
 is possible at your own risk with following implications:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
CTU CAN FD testbench accounts for two cycle delay (particularly compliance tests),
 so inserting third flip-flop will make many tests fail.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The minimal position of secondary sampling point will increment by 1 (see 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "key-1"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for description of secondary sampling point).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In case the system clock frequency,
 your technology and rate of changes on CAN bus is such that your MTBF calculations provide low values,
 rather than incrementing the number of flip-flops to 3,
 it is recommended either of:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Use dedicated synchronizer cell that has two clock cycles delay and better MTBF than two discrete flip-flops
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Put negative edge triggered flip-flop between the two flip-flops of the synchronizer by modifying the RTL.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Clock frequency
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CTU CAN FD has been synthesized to variety of FPGA families and several ASIC process nodes.
 The synthesis for FPGA is part of CTU CAN FD continous integration.
 See 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "key-4"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 for area and timing results.
 CTU CAN FD has been used on frequencies ranging from 80 - 100 MHz which is realistic target suficient for sub 100 nm ASIC PDKs and modern FPGA families.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Reset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CTU CAN FD has single external reset.
 The reset signal is named differently depending on the used memory bus wrapper (
\color red
res_n
\color inherit
,
 
\color red
hresetn
\color inherit
,
 
\color red
arstn
\color inherit
).
 For memory bus wrapper kinds the reset is assumed to be asynchrnous reset,
 and is synchronized by an internal 2 flip-flop reset synchronizer with asynchronous assert and synchronous deassert.
 The input reset signal therefore must be glitch free to prevent spurious resets.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Reset architecture
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are following types of flip-flops in CTU CAN FD:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
With asynchronous active low reset.
 These are all flip-flops apart from TXT / RX buffer memories in 
\shape italic
dp_inf_ram.vhd
\shape default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Without reset when 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_RESET_BUF_RAMS
\series default
\shape default
=false.
 These are only flip-flops in 
\shape italic
dp_inf_ram.vhd.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Design for Test
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "chap:Design-for-Test"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CTU CAN FD is design-for-test (DFT) friendly,
 and DFT has been previously inserted to CTU CAN FD.
 When inserting DFT,
 the integrator shall map top-level signal 
\color red
scan_mode
\color inherit
 to switch the design into scan mode.
 When 
\color red
scan_mode
\color inherit
=1:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
All hand instantiated clock gates are open
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
All generated resets (flip-flop A output used as asynchronous reset to flip-flop B) are replaced by the reset of the flip-flop A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Register synchronizer output is muxed and raw input reset is used on the output instead of second flip flop of the synchronizer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Memories and their testability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
CTU CAN FD contains following synchronous dual port memories with 32-bit word width:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
TXT buffer memories - 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_TXT_BUF_CNT
\series default
\shape default
 instances,
 21 word deep.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
RX buffer memories - Single instance,
 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_RX_BUF_SIZE
\series default
\shape default
 words deep.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On FPGAs,
 these memories are written to be inferred to SRAM/BRAM blocks.
 On ASIC the recommended approach differs based on:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
whether you want to insert scan chains
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
value of
\series bold
\shape italic
 G_RESET_BUF_RAMS
\series default
\shape default
 generic
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
whether you want to replace the memories by PDK specific hard macro
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Replacing memories by hard macro
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If integrator replaces the memories by hard macro,
 he shall make sure that:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The RX buffer memory matches the size given by the 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_RX_BUF_SIZE
\series default
\shape default
 generic.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The TXT buffer memory is at least 21 words deep (typically will be 32).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Used memories are dual port memories with synchronous read write.
 In case of simultaneous read and write to the same address,
 the memories return the old value.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When replacing memories by hard macro,
 value of 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_RESET_BUF_RAMS
\series default
\shape default
 generic has no effect.
 You may want to set 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_TEST_REGS_EN
\series default
\shape default
=true as this allows you to functionally test the macros (unless you insert some form of MBIST to the macro).
 If you want to insert scan chains to rest of CTU CAN FD logic,
 you will need to insert memory wrapper either in RTL or in DFT tool.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Keeping native memories
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When you do not replace memories by hard-macro,
 CTU CAN FDs memories will be synthesized out of flip-flops.
 If you set
\series bold
\shape italic
 G_RESET_BUF_RAMS
\series default
\shape default
 = true,
 the flip-flops will be reset,
 otherwise they will have no reset.
 Following approaches are reasonable in this case:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Insert scan chains to rest of CTU CAN FD logic,
 but not the flip flops of the memories.
 Insert wrappers around memories in RTL or DFT tool.
 Set 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_RESET_BUF_RAMS
\series default
\shape default
 = false and 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_TEST_REGS_EN
\series default
\shape default
=true.
 Test the memories by functional test (see the sub-chaper 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Memory-testability-support"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Insert scan chains to all CTU CAN FD logic (including memories).
 Set 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_RESET_BUF_RAMS
\series default
\shape default
 = true and 
\series bold
\shape italic
G_TEST_REGS_EN
\series default
\shape default
=false.
 Test the memories as part of ATPG scan testing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Memory testability support
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Memory-testability-support"

\end_inset


\end_layout

